<sn><md>#### Primary Succession

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-md-6
Primary succession is the `process of species colonization` in which `the habitat is initially, virtually free of life` (i.e. absent of living matter). 

The process starts with `bare rock, sand dunes, river delta, glacial dents and a newly created pond`. The first community establishing the new area is called `pioneer community`. Examples of pioneer community are `Lichens` (on rock surface), `Phyto-planktons` (pond).
:::

:::.col-12 .col-md-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Ecology%20and%20Ecotourism/03.Community%20Dynamics/resources/Primary-Succession.png)
:::
:::

**Primary Succession Stages:**

* **Pioneer Species:** Bare rock $\rightarrow$ Lichens $\rightarrow$ Small annual plants and lichens $\rightarrow$ Grasses and perennials *(hundreds of years)*
* **Intermediate Species:** Grasses, shrubs, and shade-intolerant trees such as pines
* **Climax Community:** Shade-tolerant trees such as oak and hickory
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Secondary Succession

Secondary succession `starts from a previously built-up/already existing living matter`. 

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-md-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Ecology%20and%20Ecotourism/03.Community%20Dynamics/resources/Secondary-Succession.png)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-md-6
- It is the `process of change` that `occurs after an area under colonization` has been cleared by whatsoever agency like `burning/fire, snowfall, grazing, clearing, falling of trees` etc. 
- It involves `ecosystem disruption` and the area becomes devoid of any living matter.
- Such an area will be `rapidly invaded by new populations` and they `establish a new ecosystem`. Secondary succession is `more rapid` than primary succession.
:::
:::

Secondary Succession Stages:

* **Event:** Fire *(0 years)*
* **Pioneer Species:** Annual plants *(1–2 years)* $\rightarrow$ Grasses and perennials *(3–4 years)*
* **Intermediate Species:** Grasses, shrubs, pines, young oak and hickory *(5–150 years)*
* **Climax Community:** Mature `oak and hickory` forest *(150+ years)*
</md></sn><sn><md>&gt; **Key Difference:** It is obvious that `primary succession is a rather slow process` as life has to start from nothing, whereas `secondary succession is faster` because it starts at a place which had already supported life before. Moreover, the `first species that comes into existence during primary succession` is known as ++pioneer species++.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Causes / Factors Influencing Succession

* **Climatic causes:** The climatic causes include `temperature, rainfall, light intensity, gaseous composition, wind` etc., effects on succession.
*Handwritten note:* `High rainfall and warm temperature` generally `speed up succession` process.


* **Biotic causes:** In a community, there is `competition amongst different members` for their existence. In such a process, some of the members are not found suitable and thus are gradually replaced by new ones.
* **Ecesis *(Establishment)* causes:** The `soil condition` is also changing by the process of `invasion, migration, competition and reaction` of the population.
*Handwritten note:* The speed at which a habitat changes can limit or accelerate the ability of certain species to colonize.


* **Stabilizing causes:** Succession is taking place in order to `attain the climax stage`.</md></sn>